WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.639
Picture buying a book online.

2
00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:10.658
You press Pay, and the shop's program asks another program, far away, to charge your card.

3
00:00:10.658 --> 00:00:11.595
Then it waits.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.327
How long should it wait?

5
00:00:13.327 --> 00:00:16.955
And when it gives up, does the far side stop working too?

6
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:22.438
In your own code, a function call returns, or it throws, faster than you can blink.

7
00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:26.451
A call to another computer can do a third thing: nothing at all.

8
00:00:26.451 --> 00:00:30.715
The question gets lost, or the answer does, and no reply ever comes.

9
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:32.855
Let's give the programs names.

10
00:00:32.855 --> 00:00:34.807
Checkout is the shop's program.

11
00:00:34.807 --> 00:00:40.096
It calls payments, which calls the issuer, the bank that gave the customer the card.

12
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:49.267
They talk with gRPC, a free tool from Google that lets one program call a function inside another program, over the network.

13
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:51.166
And here's the catch.

14
00:00:51.166 --> 00:00:54.510
gRPC sets no time limit unless you set one.

15
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.087
So if a reply is lost, checkout waits forever.

16
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:02.497
While it waits, it holds a thread, one path of work in the program.

17
00:01:02.497 --> 00:01:05.825
Lose enough replies, and the shop runs out of threads.

18
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.157
Forever is a long time, even for a computer.

19
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:12.931
What we want is a rule: wait this long, and no longer.

20
00:01:12.931 --> 00:01:16.900
And when we stop waiting, everyone down the line should stop too.

21
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:18.579
Think of a phone call.

22
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:21.147
You ring a bike shop and ask for a part.

23
00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:23.523
You say: I can hold for five minutes.

24
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:26.764
The clerk puts you on hold and phones the warehouse.

25
00:01:26.764 --> 00:01:29.645
A good clerk says: I need it within three minutes.

26
00:01:29.645 --> 00:01:32.469
Two minutes have gone already, so three are left.

27
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.494
A careless clerk says nothing.

28
00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:40.415
So the warehouse climbs a ladder and searches for ten minutes, for a customer who hung up long ago.

29
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:41.814
Let's map it.

30
00:01:41.814 --> 00:01:43.053
You are checkout.

31
00:01:43.053 --> 00:01:44.656
The clerk is payments.

32
00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:46.696
The warehouse is the issuer.

33
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.944
And "I can hold for five minutes" is the deadline: the moment after which the caller stops waiting.

34
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:56.284
Now the real numbers, from the lesson's demo.

35
00:01:56.284 --> 00:02:00.351
Checkout calls payments with a deadline of 300 milliseconds.

36
00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:08.138
Payments does 120 milliseconds of its own work, then calls the issuer, and sets no deadline of its own.

37
00:02:08.138 --> 00:02:09.959
The issuer is slow today.

38
00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:11.853
It needs 500 milliseconds.

39
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.283
So when the issuer's call arrives, how much time does it see left?

40
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:17.741
And who gives up first?

41
00:02:17.741 --> 00:02:18.755
Pause and guess.

42
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:24.051
The fair guess goes like this.

43
00:02:24.051 --> 00:02:27.455
Payments set no deadline, so the issuer has none.

44
00:02:27.455 --> 00:02:29.261
At 300, checkout gives up.

45
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:32.464
But the issuer works on until about 627.

46
00:02:32.464 --> 00:02:37.740
A third of a second of work for nobody, holding a thread the whole time.

47
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.132
The careless clerk.

48
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.836
Now the real run.

49
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.742
Payments gets the call with 299 milliseconds left.

50
00:02:45.742 --> 00:02:51.365
At 127, the issuer gets its call, with 173 milliseconds left.

51
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.999
Payments never set a deadline, and still the issuer knew.

52
00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:58.266
At 311, checkout gets deadline exceeded.

53
00:02:58.266 --> 00:03:02.841
One millisecond later, payments' call to the issuer ends too.

54
00:03:02.841 --> 00:03:07.191
And at 321, the issuer sees its call cancelled, and stops.

55
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:10.149
So the library was the good clerk all along.

56
00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:12.311
Here's how it travels.

57
00:03:12.311 --> 00:03:16.828
Every call carries headers, lines of extra detail sent with it.

58
00:03:16.828 --> 00:03:23.425
The deadline rides in one named grpc-timeout, as time remaining, not as a time on the clock.

59
00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:28.578
Why not send a clock time, like "stop at 12 o'clock and 300 milliseconds"?

60
00:03:28.578 --> 00:03:31.829
Because two machines' clocks never agree exactly.

61
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.925
"I can hold for five minutes" works whatever each watch says.

62
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:47.492
grpc-java, gRPC's library for Java and Kotlin, and its library for the Go language, pass the time that's left on to the next call by themselves.

63
00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:53.384
When it runs out, the caller gets deadline exceeded, and the server's side of the call is cancelled.

64
00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:58.830
In code, it's one setting on the call: with deadline after, 300, milliseconds.

65
00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.628
And payments' own call to the issuer needs nothing extra.

66
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.390
Three rules.

67
00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:09.749
One: gRPC sets no deadline unless you do, so give every call one.

68
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:12.283
Two: Java and Go pass it on for you.

69
00:04:12.283 --> 00:04:16.532
With another library, check, and pass it on yourself if you have to.

70
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.525
Three: deadline exceeded means nobody knows what happened.

71
00:04:20.525 --> 00:04:23.800
The card may have been charged just before the end.

72
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.855
So calling again isn't safe yet.

73
00:04:25.855 --> 00:04:30.608
Later in this lesson, a retry gets a key, so it can't charge a card twice.

74
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.851
So how long should a call wait?

75
00:04:32.851 --> 00:04:35.433
As long as its caller can, and no longer.

76
00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:37.384
Give the first call a deadline.

77
00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:43.314
It travels down the chain as time remaining, and when it runs out, everyone stops together.

78
00:04:43.314 --> 00:04:46.436
No warehouse searching for a customer who hung up.

79
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.056
One quick check.

80
00:04:48.056 --> 00:04:50.729
Checkout sets 300 milliseconds again.

81
00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:55.135
This time, payments works for 250 before it calls the issuer.

82
00:04:55.135 --> 00:04:58.386
About how much time does the issuer see left?

83
00:04:58.386 --> 00:04:59.541
Pause and think.

84
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:04.889
About 50 milliseconds.

85
00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:08.123
300, minus the 250 already spent.
